.TH RECIPE-RECIPE 5 "2026-11-16" "Recipe" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
recipe-recipe \- the API 1 Rhai recipe format
.SH DESCRIPTION
A recipe is a Rhai file. It states facts in literal declarations and writes
code only for what is unusual. Recipe downloads, checks, unpacks, places files,
unpacks distribution packages, finishes system trees, records what is done,
installs, removes and cleans up. docs/recipe-format.md is the full contract and
WRITING_RECIPES.md the practical guide; this page is a summary.
.PP
The first line of every recipe, and of every base a recipe extends, is
const RECIPE_API = 1; the engine accepts that number and nothing else. Every
recipe, and every child of a base, also declares a literal const NEEDS. There are
no hooks and no ctx: a recipe that defines a function named acquire, install,
remove, is_acquired, is_built or is_installed, or declares ctx or RUNTIME, is
refused with the line to change.
.PP
"Literal" means plain data: no helper calls, no variables, no arithmetic. Recipe
reads every declaration without running the recipe (list, info, index,
validate, the pin generator and the app do).
.SH DECLARATIONS
.TP
.B NEEDS
Required. inputs (name to "file", "directory", "file?" or "directory?"), defines
(name to its default; () means required), outputs (name to "file" or
"directory"), network ("host:port" endpoints, redirect hosts included),
network_max_bytes (default 8 GiB), memory_mib (2048), storage_max_objects
(16384), storage_max_bytes and timeout_secs (3600, 1 to 86400). Callers can only
narrow it. outputs holds one name in a normal recipe (package, system, tools or
a product name); the body never uses it. Every host of a SOURCES URL, redirects
included, must be in network.
.TP
.B ABOUT
What a front end shows, checked for form only: kind, category, libc, libc_carried,
systems, details, homepage, license, adds, steps (keys download, build, install)
and keeps. ABOUT.libc and ABOUT.systems drive the \-\-any\-libc and
\-\-any\-system advice.
.TP
.B PACKAGE
name, version and description, literal strings. name equals the file name
without .rhai and may be left out. version is required for a package or system
output, except for a recipe with PACKAGES, whose version is the pins file's
version line.
.TP
.B SOURCES
A map from a source name to url and sha256, or git and commit; optional file,
unpack (false keeps one file), format (tar.gz, tar.xz, tar.bz2, tar.zst, zip or
rpm), strip (false keeps a single top folder), libc ("glibc" or "musl": fetched
only on that C library) and tag (informational). Downloads go into Recipe's
download store, named by SHA-256, offline cache first. In build, src.NAME is the
path of the unpacked folder, the single file or the checkout. A single top
folder of an archive is left out unless strip is false.
.TP
.B FILES
A list of [from, to] or [from, to, mode]. from starts with the source's name
(left out when there is exactly one source; a plain-file source is always named).
A from ending in / is a whole folder ("NAME/" the whole source, "" with one
source); a to ending in / or "" is a folder. A single file without a mode gets
0644; files of a whole folder keep their modes. In a recipe whose output is a
file, the entry with to "" makes the output. Files are moved into the output;
a from that does not exist fails the run. FILES are placed before build runs.
.TP
.B PACKAGES
Distribution package seeds for the pin generator: a map from glibc (Fedora RPMs),
musl (Alpine APKs) or glibc_i686 to a list, or a plain list for every system.
Recipe reads only the pins file NAME.pins beside the recipe (written by the
generator, never edited by hand; part of the identity).
.TP
.B ACCOUNTS
A map from C library to sysusers.d u, g and m lines, only with a system output,
for accounts the packages' own sysusers.d does not make.
.TP
.B UPSTREAM
github (OWNER/REPO, newest release that is not a pre-release) and optional
prefix (text taken off the tag). Asked only by recipe updates \-\-upstream.
.TP
.B RERUN
const RERUN = true; the recipe runs every time it is asked for and is never
"built".
.TP
.B deps, build_deps
let lists of recipe names. deps stay installed; build_deps are there only while
the recipe builds. Each runs under its own NEEDS and reaches the consumer
through DEPS.
.TP
.B extends, import
//! extends: base.rhai on the first line makes the file a child (one level). A
function the child defines replaces the base's of the same name and arity; a
declaration is taken whole from the child, else whole from the base; NEEDS must be
in the child. import "path.rhai" as name; has a literal path relative to the
recipe's folder; a module has no RECIPE_API and no NEEDS.
.SH FUNCTIONS
.TP
.B build(src, out)
The recipe's own work. src maps source names to paths (src.packages with
PACKAGES: a list in pin order of name, path, sha256, bytes, var, licence). out is
the output folder (placed FILES inside; a copy of the target input) or, for a
file output, the path to make; with several outputs a map from output name to
path. It returns nothing. May start programs (then the recipe runs in Ginux),
download when the recipe has no SOURCES, and use WORK.
.TP
.B configure(tree) or configure(tree, src)
Only with PACKAGES and no build. Called after the packages are unpacked into the
tree and FILES placed; writes the group's own configuration with put, put_link,
tree_text and enable_service. The second argument is the other SOURCES.
.TP
.B verify(out)
Optional read-only check of the result after build or configure. Starts no
program. A throw fails the run. Not run again later.
.TP
.B latest()
Returns the newest upstream version as a string. Runs alone on the host, starts no
program, sees no inputs and reaches only NEEDS.network.
.TP
.B cleanup(reason)
Optional. reason is manual, auto.PHASE.success or auto.PHASE.failure, where PHASE
is download, build or install. Called once at the end of a run after the engine's
own clean-up; must not delete installed files.
.PP
A recipe may define other functions and constants. Functions see their arguments,
the engine's constants, the recipe's own constants whose value is literal data,
its declarations and imported modules (through their alias); a top-level value
computed with a call is not visible inside a function. Statements outside
functions, other than declarations, run once per run before the first function,
only for steps that run recipe code.
.SH CONSTANTS
Read-only, visible everywhere:
.TP
.B PREFIX
Where a package output will live once activated; () with \-\-no-activate, for a
dependency and for other outputs.
.TP
.B JOBS
The jobs the user asked for, 1 to 16.
.TP
.B HOST_LIBC
glibc, musl or unknown: the C library of the system the result is installed on
(the root written into, for a system change).
.TP
.B PACKAGE_FORMAT
rpm or apk, the format of the pins in use; () without PACKAGES.
.TP
.B INPUTS, DEFINES, DEPS
Maps: each declared input to its read-only path (() when an optional input was not
given); each declared define to its value; each dependency to the path of its
output (a map from output name to path for a dependency with several outputs).
.TP
.B WORK
A private folder for build material, deleted after a successful run unless kept.
.SH STEPS, STATES AND SAVED VALUES
Four steps: download, build, install and cleanup (see recipe(1)). install does
every missing step, then cleanup unless the keep option is on. The state of a
program (none, downloaded, built, installed) is Recipe's own record; no recipe code
runs to decide it. After a successful install the downloads, the unpacked
sources, WORK and the saved state are deleted.
.PP
A recipe that must remember a value across an interruption calls save(name,
value), saved(name), checkpoint(name) and reached(name) (see recipe-helpers(7)).
A value is saved only together with the files of its checkpoint, a saved value
never proves that work is done, and Recipe keeps one checkpoint at most.
checkpoint works only inside build and configure. An interrupted build resumes from
the newest checkpoint: Recipe restores it and calls build(src, out) again from
the top; the recipe skips what reached(name) says is done.
.SH SYSTEM OUTPUTS
A system output is a root-relative tree that recipe \-\-system writes into a root
with a receipt. Recipe finishes it after configure or build: it removes the folders
a system package does not own (run, tmp, boot, home, root, proc, sys, dev,
lost+found, var/run, var/tmp, var/lock) and finalizes the tree with owners resolved
in the root written into, var/ as state seeds and ACCOUNTS. A recipe does not call
target_finalize on it. Trees for images (rootfs, overlay, medium) are not system
outputs; their recipes finalize them. See docs/target-metadata.md.
.SH INPUTS AND OUTPUTS
Callers bind exactly declared inputs with \-\-input NAME=PATH, read-only;
DEP::NAME binds a dependency input. An input named target is the caller's existing
tree and seeds the output before build runs. Defines select genuine recipe
choices (DEFINES.NAME); a define given on the command line reaches every recipe of
the graph that declares it, and an undeclared define is refused. Names beginning
with RECIPE_ are the engine's. Defines grant no host access.
.PP
Archive modification times have whole-second precision. Target ownership, special
modes and capabilities travel through the validated target metadata manifest. A
package output refuses setuid and setgid bits. Successful execution plus validated,
bounded archive publication returns absolute host paths in the result's outputs.
.SH ACQUISITION
Prefer SOURCES. Inside build, a recipe with no SOURCES may call download(url,
exact_dest, sha256[, resume_bool]), download_exact, fetch_bounded and
git_checkout(url, exact_dest, full_commit) within NEEDS.network; the full SHA256
and a full 40/64-hex commit are required. Every redirect host needs a grant.
Explicit restrictions from the caller cap every dependency's own limits and
intersect its endpoints; no grant is lent.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
const RECIPE_API = 1;
const NEEDS = #{
    outputs: #{ "package": "directory" },
    network: ["github.com:443", "release-assets.githubusercontent.com:443"],
};
const PACKAGE = #{ name: "ripgrep", version: "15.2.0", description: "Fast recursive text search with rg" };
const UPSTREAM = #{ github: "BurntSushi/ripgrep" };
const SOURCES = #{
    release: #{
        url: "https://github.com/BurntSushi/ripgrep/releases/download/15.2.0/ripgrep-15.2.0-x86_64-unknown-linux-musl.tar.gz",
        sha256: "33e15bcf1624b25cdd2a55813a47a2f95dbe126268203e76aa6a585d1e7b149c",
    },
};
const FILES = [
    ["rg", "bin/rg", 0o755],
    ["doc/rg.1", "share/man/man1/rg.1"],
];
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
recipe(1), recipe-helpers(7), WRITING_RECIPES.md, docs/recipe-format.md
